1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,659
Shine a laser on a wall, and the spot sparkles

2
00:00:03,699 --> 00:00:07,425
with grainy speckle. An LED's spot is smooth.

3
00:00:07,495 --> 00:00:09,771
Through two slits, the laser paints crisp fringes.

4
00:00:09,811 --> 00:00:14,881
The LED paints none, unless it first shines through a tiny pinhole.

5
00:00:14,951 --> 00:00:19,173
What does a laser have, that an LED doesn't? In a word: coherence.

6
00:00:19,993 --> 00:00:23,804
At any point, a light wave is an arrow, a complex number.

7
00:00:23,844 --> 00:00:26,810
Its length is the amplitude, its angle the phase.

8
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:29,643
It spins hundreds of trillions of times a second,

9
00:00:29,683 --> 00:00:31,102
too fast for any camera,

10
00:00:31,142 --> 00:00:34,643
which records only its squared length: the intensity.

11
00:00:34,713 --> 00:00:38,203
Where two waves meet, their arrows add. They spin together,

12
00:00:38,243 --> 00:00:41,086
so only the angle between them matters: Δφ.

13
00:00:41,156 --> 00:00:43,701
Squaring the sum gives both intensities,

14
00:00:43,741 --> 00:00:47,448
plus a cross term that depends on Δφ: interference.

15
00:00:47,518 --> 00:00:50,066
Move across a screen, and Δφ grows steadily,

16
00:00:50,106 --> 00:00:52,645
so the cross term paints fringes.

17
00:00:52,715 --> 00:00:54,984
Now let one wave's phase jitter randomly,

18
00:00:55,024 --> 00:00:58,482
faster than the camera can follow. The fringes dance.

19
00:00:58,552 --> 00:01:02,105
If averaging removes the cross term, the intensities add.

20
00:01:02,975 --> 00:01:04,480
The visibility measures what's left:

21
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:07,696
one for perfect fringes, zero for none.

22
00:01:08,516 --> 00:01:11,199
Why would phases wander? One reason is colour.

23
00:01:11,239 --> 00:01:15,402
Split a beam, and delay one copy by a path difference, ΔL.

24
00:01:15,472 --> 00:01:18,394
With a single colour, the fringes go on forever.

25
00:01:18,464 --> 00:01:20,608
But each colour has its own spacing.

26
00:01:20,648 --> 00:01:24,256
At zero delay they agree; further out, they drift apart.

27
00:01:24,326 --> 00:01:29,735
Add a whole spectrum, and fringes survive only near zero delay.

28
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:33,506
That window is the coherence length, roughly λ²/Δλ.

29
00:01:33,576 --> 00:01:36,924
In fact, the envelope is the Fourier transform of the spectrum:

30
00:01:36,964 --> 00:01:39,773
narrow spectrum, long coherence.

31
00:01:39,843 --> 00:01:42,988
A good laser: centimetres to kilometres. An LED:

32
00:01:43,028 --> 00:01:47,292
about ten microns. Sunlight: about one micron.

33
00:01:48,112 --> 00:01:49,639
Colour isn't the whole story.

34
00:01:49,679 --> 00:01:52,536
A laser behaves like a single point of light;

35
00:01:52,576 --> 00:01:56,120
an LED is a crowd of independent points.

36
00:01:56,190 --> 00:01:59,670
Move one point off centre. Its light reaches the slits tilted,

37
00:01:59,710 --> 00:02:02,632
and the tilt shifts its fringes sideways.

38
00:02:02,702 --> 00:02:04,517
Different points have unrelated phases,

39
00:02:04,557 --> 00:02:09,160
so their intensities add. The camera sees a sum of shifted copies.

40
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:12,624
Watch as the source grows: the copies spread out and fill

41
00:02:12,664 --> 00:02:16,067
in each other's dark bands, until the sum is flat grey.

42
00:02:16,937 --> 00:02:20,455
Fringes vanish once the source's angular size

43
00:02:20,495 --> 00:02:22,874
reaches about λ over the slit separation.

44
00:02:22,944 --> 00:02:25,597
A tiny pinhole brings them back. So does distance:

45
00:02:25,637 --> 00:02:28,867
a giant star, far away, looks tiny.

46
00:02:28,937 --> 00:02:32,571
How visibility fades as the slits move apart is the Fourier

47
00:02:32,611 --> 00:02:36,696
transform of the source's shape: the van Cittert–Zernike theorem.

48
00:02:36,766 --> 00:02:39,417
In 1920, Michelson spread two mirrors

49
00:02:39,457 --> 00:02:41,817
apart until a star's fringes vanished,

50
00:02:41,857 --> 00:02:44,277
and measured the size of Betelgeuse.

51
00:02:45,097 --> 00:02:49,590
Coherence has a dark side. A wall is rough on the scale of a wavelength.

52
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:53,276
At each pixel, light arrives from every bump with a random phase,

53
00:02:53,316 --> 00:02:56,251
and the arrows add up like a random walk.

54
00:02:56,321 --> 00:02:58,560
Sometimes they cancel, sometimes they line up:

55
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,272
dark and bright grains called speckle.

56
00:03:01,342 --> 00:03:05,715
Its contrast, the standard deviation over the mean, is exactly one.

57
00:03:05,785 --> 00:03:09,935
Each grain is about λ/NA across: the numerical aperture,

58
00:03:09,975 --> 00:03:13,519
NA, is the sine of the widest angle collected.

59
00:03:13,589 --> 00:03:17,965
Average N independent patterns, and the contrast falls as 1/√N.

60
00:03:18,005 --> 00:03:21,784
An LED's many colours and points do just that.

61
00:03:22,604 --> 00:03:25,559
How do we compute with partially coherent light? Record,

62
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:29,399
for every pair of points, how well their fields keep in step.

63
00:03:29,469 --> 00:03:33,950
That's the mutual intensity, J: the average of the field at x₁

64
00:03:33,990 --> 00:03:39,097
times the conjugate field at x₂. Its diagonal is the intensity.

65
00:03:39,167 --> 00:03:41,607
Off the diagonal, it says how strongly two

66
00:03:41,647 --> 00:03:44,044
points would interfere through a double slit.

67
00:03:44,114 --> 00:03:45,961
Swapping the points conjugates J,

68
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:49,788
so it's a Hermitian matrix, and it can be diagonalized.

69
00:03:49,858 --> 00:03:51,865
Its eigenvectors are coherent modes,

70
00:03:51,905 --> 00:03:56,371
each perfectly coherent, and its eigenvalues are their powers.

71
00:03:56,441 --> 00:03:59,398
So partially coherent light is a sum of independent

72
00:03:59,438 --> 00:04:02,259
coherent fields, whose intensities add.

73
00:04:03,129 --> 00:04:07,384
A laser needs one mode. The bigger the source, the more modes it takes.

74
00:04:08,204 --> 00:04:10,303
On a computer, that gives three recipes.

75
00:04:10,343 --> 00:04:13,822
Coherent light: propagate the field, then square it.

76
00:04:13,892 --> 00:04:17,317
Incoherent light: add intensities, often with a convolution.

77
00:04:17,357 --> 00:04:19,264
Partially coherent light:

78
00:04:19,304 --> 00:04:22,439
propagate a few modes, and add their intensities.

79
00:04:22,509 --> 00:04:24,720
Here's a diffractive element lit by a laser.

80
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:28,151
Its far-field pattern is crisp, but speckled.

81
00:04:28,221 --> 00:04:32,098
With an LED, each source point tilts the light, shifting the pattern,

82
00:04:32,138 --> 00:04:36,138
and each colour stretches it. The sum is blurred, but smooth.

83
00:04:36,208 --> 00:04:39,472
At large angles, this simple blur stops being exact,

84
00:04:39,512 --> 00:04:42,249
but a few coherent modes are often enough.

85
00:04:43,069 --> 00:04:45,710
Fields always add; the exposure determines

86
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:47,507
which interference terms survive.

87
00:04:47,547 --> 00:04:51,480
When the averaged cross term vanishes, intensities add.

88
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:55,144
And partially coherent light is a sum of coherent patterns.

89
00:04:55,214 --> 00:04:58,331
Next time: coherence lets us do what a camera can't,

90
00:04:58,371 --> 00:05:00,212
and record the phase of light.

